using static StrataLint.Scribe.DefinitionDsl;

namespace StrataLint.Scribe.Blueprint.D5.S3.Geometry.AlgebraicBoundary;

internal sealed class ConeMinimumBranchDocument : IScribeDocumentDefinition
{
    public DocumentDefinition Create() => DocumentDefinition.Create(ScribeNode.Create(
        "Actual low-degree cone minima, roots and entire closed branches.",
        H("Actual low-degree cone minima, roots and entire closed branches."),
        Blocks(
            Paragraph(Text("For an arbitrary nonzero finite-degree equation of the entire original boundary, BoundaryComponentRecovery identifies the cone with the closure of the union of the connected components of its nonzero locus lying in the original interior. The proof allows polynomial zeros inside the cone. This equality supplies the geometric recovery step; semialgebraic finiteness of those components and closure stability are additional requirements for a semialgebraicity conclusion.")),
            Paragraph(Text("For every strictly positive original unit u, retain the entire affine section K={x in C: u(x)=1}. At every relative interior center a, the actual translated body A in ker(u) has inscribed and circumscribed closed balls of radii 0<r<R. Its gauge satisfies both norm comparisons and the difference estimate with constant 1/r on all vectors. The entire unit sphere is homeomorphic to the entire relative frontier of K by the exact map v to a+v/gauge(A,v). This map has distance bound R+R squared/r. These statements hold for the original normalization and every relative interior center, without symmetry or smoothness. The geometric construction is given by ConeRadialGeometry.section_radial_geometry.")),
            Paragraph(Text("For each original strictly positive linear unit, K is compact and P is exactly its full set of extreme points. The proper interval consists of every linear functional f satisfying 0<=f(x)<=u(x) on every x in C. Finite dimension identifies these with continuous linear functionals without restricting the interval. Full dimensionality of the original cone gives its linear spanning property, so ContinuousFrameSum.effects_compact applies to that same interval. For every p in P, its entire confirming family is the closed slice f(p)=1 and is compact. ExclusionWidthCompactness.comparison_attained supplies an actual minimizing confirmer at every original ambient vector x. In particular this includes every mixed state, the vertex and every nonnegative cone mass. At a cone input the minimum lies between zero and u(x), and on K its zero locus agrees with the existing existential exclusion set. No least single confirmer, reciprocity, exclusion-ball condition or physical realization is assumed or concluded from the boundary hypothesis.")),
            Paragraph(Text("For a homogeneous minimum entire-boundary equation, absence of interior zeros follows from the actual compact base. The strictly positive normalization, its normalized interior point and the kernel coordinate equivalence are constructed from the original closed salient full cone. Exact degree and minimum correspondence transfer the minimum equation to the compact body, where an interior zero would yield a lower-degree entire-boundary equation. In the zero-dimensional kernel case every original vector is a scalar multiple of the normalized point, and nonzero homogeneity excludes an interior zero directly. Continuity on the convex original interior then supplies one common sign. The analytic branch argument uses these derived facts. For a point x in C and t<0, x+(-t)e lies in the original interior, so homogeneity excludes q_x(t)=0. Conversely, if x is outside C, the negative-cone ray endpoint gives an actual negative root; on the axis, the root s itself is negative. This yields equality between C and the entire set of points whose q_x roots are all nonnegative. Every interior direction and every original vector are retained. The original interior is exactly the connected component of the polynomial nonzero set containing the direction, and its closure is the original closed cone. Any other nonzero entire-boundary equation of the same minimum total degree is homogeneous: every lower homogeneous component vanishes on the entire frontier and would contradict minimum degree. Subtract a scalar multiple of the selected minimum equation to cancel its value at an interior point. If the difference were nonzero it would again be a minimum equation with an interior zero. Hence the original minimum equation is unique up to a nonzero real scalar. The analytic argument concerns minimum degree at most three. The separate arbitrary-degree minimum_boundary_ideal theorem proves that every polynomial vanishing on the entire original boundary is divisible by this same q, with no degree bound or homogeneity assumption on the comparison equation. Its converse follows by evaluation. Thus q is the actual homogeneous squarefree principal generator of the entire boundary ideal, and its total degree is the original minimum. This identification uses the original carrier transport and the actual finite-degree minimum, rather than defining a new name for a boundary-covering equation.")),
            Paragraph(Text("In a one-dimensional original carrier, the constructed normalization u and normalized interior point b satisfy x=u(x)b for every vector. The cone is exactly u(x) nonnegative and its entire frontier is the original vertex. The polynomial representing u is homogeneous of total degree one. A polynomial vanishes on that entire frontier exactly when this linear polynomial divides it, by the actual hyperplane factorization. Thus the ray case has its actual principal boundary ideal, rather than a boundary-covering surrogate.")),
            Paragraph(Text("For the converse, suppose a homogeneous polynomial is nonzero at an original interior direction and its whole closed nonnegative-root branch is exactly the original cone. At a boundary point where the polynomial were nonzero, the actual line polynomial would have no zero root. Every real root is positive because the point belongs to the branch. Its finite set of roots has a strictly positive common lower bound epsilon, including the case of an empty root set. Translating the point backwards by epsilon times the direction shifts every real root by minus epsilon and therefore stays in the same whole branch. Adding that positive multiple of the interior direction then puts the original boundary point in the interior, a contradiction. Consequently this same polynomial vanishes on the entire original boundary. This uses the genuine whole-branch equality; hyperbolicity plus boundary covering alone does not supply it.")))));
}
